From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ram: a word's write enable and hold, buffered per eight bits
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

With -write_style mux every bit of a word has a hold mux selected by the
word's write enable and by its complement, the hold. Both were one net
across the whole word: a 32-bit word's enable, from an AND2, drove 33
pins and its hold, from one inverter, the word's 32 hold muxes. In a
flow every storage bit then missed the period at placement and
repair_timing spent its time buffering them back.

Each group of eight bits now takes a buffered copy of the word's enable
and makes its own hold from it, in the group's first bit's cell: the
word's enable drives a buffer a group, and no net in a word drives more
than a group's pins.

Carried in bazel-orfs, not upstreamed; retires with the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
